\section{A Binary Decision for Each Match}

Start with instantiated instructions rather than with an opcode catalog. Let $F_m$ be the set of IR operations implemented by candidate $m$, $c_m$ its cost, and $x_m\in\{0,1\}$ its selection variable. In a model with all operations required and no recomputation, an integer linear program begins with
\[
  \min \sum_m c_m x_m,\qquad
  \sum_{m:\,n\in F_m} x_m = 1
  \quad\text{for every required operation }n.
\]
Blindell describes Wilson and colleagues' integrated integer programming approach, where the corresponding ownership constraint is at most one because nodes may become inactive. In that setting, a separate activation or demand condition is needed for required operations; using at most one alone would permit selecting nothing. Blindell also distinguishes designs that assume precomputed matches from Bednarski and Kessler's formulation that uses variables to map pattern nodes and edges as well as to select instructions \cite[chapter~4]{blindell2016instruction}. Adding match variables changes what the solver has to discover. An exact solver on a pruned match list guarantees only the best solution in that list.

For every non-live-in input to a chosen match, an additional constraint can require a compatible producer. If $P$ is the list of candidates actually exporting the value in an acceptable form, $x_m\leq\sum_{p\in P}x_p$ requires one of them when $m$ is selected. A model can extend this with explicit copies, or link producer and consumer choices to times and registers. Those variables matter if selected instructions cannot all be scheduled or if transfers change the cost. Thus the objective's name should say what it measures: for example, minimum bytes among legal covers under a fixed register-class model, not minimum execution time.

\section{Finite Domains and All-or-Nothing Matches}

A constraint programming (CP) model can give each required operation $n$ a finite-domain variable $z_n$ ranging over the IDs of the candidates that own it. It must not allow different operations of a multi-output candidate to select that candidate independently. In the approach Blindell attributes to Floch and colleagues, for each candidate $m$ count the occurrences of its ID among the variables for $F_m$. Restrict the count to $\{0,|F_m|\}$. Every node chooses exactly one ID, and a positive count forces the candidate to own its whole footprint. Charge the cost once when the count is nonzero, not once per operation.

Propagation can eliminate a match from every node in its footprint as soon as it has become unavailable at one node. A finite-domain model can also use a table of legal operand assignments or a resource constraint across several operations without spelling those out as individual linear inequalities. This does not mean all CP models propagate equally well; splitting a global constraint into weaker pieces may hide consequences until search makes more assignments. Both CP and integer linear programming can model the candidate-selection problem, whose general budget decision version is NP-complete. Computing the cheapest solution is NP-hard regardless of the notation used to ask for it.

A common modeling mistake is to use an objective increment as a substitute for a missing legality condition. Suppose a consumer in one register bank needs a producer in another. Adding a copy charge does not produce a copy unless that transfer is allowed and participates in the returned plan. This is especially visible if the copy occupies a functional unit or has an effect on placement. Blindell notes that several of the CP designs he surveys omit transfers between register classes. Their solver can be exact and their objective still miss costs on heterogeneous targets. Check the selected plan against an independent legality checker before treating any claimed optimum as a baseline.

