\section{Fractional Pairs Are Not Instructions}

Three mandatory operations read the same eight-bit live-in $t$. They produce $a=t\mathbin{\mathtt{xor}}\mathtt{0x11}$, $b=t+\mathtt{0x23}$ modulo 256, and $c$ by rotating $t$ left three bits. The toy target can compute any one result for two cost units or any pair in one instruction for three. Every result is live-out and all candidates require only $t$. There are no other instructions or resource restrictions.

\begin{verbatim}
t = 0x24 -> (a,b,c) = (0x35,0x47,0x21)
LP: x_ab = x_bc = x_ac = 1/2, all singles = 0
    # Each ownership row sums to 1, but cost 4.5 isn't code.
CP: choose z_a = ab -> force z_b = ab
    forbid ac and bc -> choose z_c = c
    # The ab match is paid once: 3+2 = 5.
\end{verbatim}

Relaxing the binary variables to the interval $[0,1]$ gives a linear programming (LP) lower bound. Here the sum of ownership rows is $s+2p=3$, where $s$ is the sum of the singleton variables and $p$ the sum of the pair variables. The cost is $2s+3p=6-p$, and nonnegative $s$ gives $p\leq1.5$. Thus $4.5$ is the relaxation optimum, attained by the three half-pairs. In the integer model at most one pair can be used: any two pairs overlap. A pair and a singleton give five, all singletons give six. The rounded-up LP lower bound of five is valid because integral selections have integral costs; it is not obtained by rounding the variables. A verified incumbent of cost five and that bound certify optimality. On larger instances a solver may stop with a gap between its bound and incumbent; the incumbent is usable code only after its selected matches and constraints have been checked.

The inequality $p\leq1$ also strengthens the relaxation without eliminating any integer solution. It captures a fact about all three pair candidates together that the original ownership equations missed in the fractional case.

